00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
well let's talk to the shadow foreign  secretary lisa nandy good to see you  this morning  so grant shap says the labour party is  obsessed with all of this when you  should be focusing on  well the pandemic for a kickoff well we  are focused on the pandemic but we also  want to know that we have a prime  minister  in this country who puts the interests  of the british people first and the  problem  with what has gone on over the flats is  that we still don't know who  loaned the prime minister this money he  won't even confirm or deny  whether it was loaned or not we don't  know why he didn't declare it and we  don't know what he  offered them in return the reason that  those rules are there is so that we know  who powerful figures are beholden to who  they may owe favors to  so that we can properly scrutinize and  check that they're not acting in the  interests of a privileged few  over and above the majority of people in  this country and at the moment we do not  have a prime minister in this country  that we can be confident is acting in  the interest of the british people  that's just unacceptable  and yet it seems to be if there if there  was any money  lent for this it seems to have come from  cchq doesn't it from the conservative  party itself in effect  we have absolutely no idea and that is  the problem that  days after these allegations surfaced we  still  haven't got any clarity from the  government from the prime minister  about what has gone on his press  secretary said that there was  a donation made a loan made he said that  there wasn't  somebody is lying here and we have to  have a government that is prepared to be  open and honest with the british people  and is prepared  to tell the truth over the last few  weeks what we've seen is a  mass of revelations about the very murky  financial dealings and cosy  relationships at the heart  of government we've got a situation  where the crown prince of saudi arabia  can pick up the phone to the prime  minister  and try and subvert processes that are  meant to protect football fans  in this country from the sort of  nightmare scenario that we went through  in wigan with the collapse of wigan  athletic and yet  we there are ordinary people as gordon  brown just said  on your programme who can't pick up the  phone to the prime minister who don't  have the health secretary's  mobile number who can't just text the  chancellor to advance their own  financial interests  it's one rule for them and one rule for  the rest of us  there's always going to be a case though  isn't there where where those people who  have  jobs of influence have access to  politicians i mean you know that  when the people who who come forward to  to anyone in a position of power you  have  better access than than than someone who  lives on worcester street  in barrow in furnace i'm not not sure  that's right actually i mean you know i  i have  social workers in my constituency deputy  had teachers in my constituency who have  my mobile number  and can pick up the phone when they need  something but i do agree  with the central point which is that  there will always be relationships in  government  there'll be relationships financial  relationships as well as  political relationships and power  relationships and that's why we have  these rules  about declaring donations about being  transparent and honest  so that people can be assured that there  isn't one rule for them  and another rule for the rest of us the  bottom line here  is integrity isn't it whether it's does  it have to be real integrity or  perceived integrity i think the bottom  line is  integrity it's honesty and it's  accountability and on  all three of those measures this prime  minister is falling  short we've got to see him come to the  house of commons make a statement  about this come clean about who  offered him money who he took money from  and what he owes them  in return why hasn't he declared these  donations  we've got to start to clean up this  sleaze at the heart of government  it's interesting when i was talking to  to gordon brown now he was  he was very careful not to suggest  sakira should follow  a different path on this but he was very  clear very clear within the interview  that  you know he thought this was yeah an  issue that that  was nowhere near as important to say  this the situation in india and the  global need  to tackle the pandemic you know the idea  that we need to now  take a global view on the pandemic i  mean  what do you take from that do do you  think you are pushing a domestic issue  when perhaps it is not the time to do so  no i think honesty trust and integrity  are absolutely vital  in a democracy otherwise the system  can't survive it collapses  so i don't see any any  um dissonance between wanting to focus  on the situation in india and wanting to  see the government step  up and wanting to see a government that  acts with integrity  that the british people can trust the  two things are synonymous they go  absolutely hand in hand that's why boris  johnson will face questions i'm sure at  prime minister's questions  later today about why he has behaved in  the dishonest  way that he has but it's also why  dominic raab the foreign secretary will  face questions from me later today  about why we're not doing more to tackle  what is now a global emergency no longer  a domestic crisis in india  and what do you want to see the foreign  secretary do in in terms of india  because we're talking of them surpassing  200 000 deaths i mean the likelihood is  it is  probably many times more that absolutely  i mean there are two million confirmed  cases in  india don't forget this is a country  that is 20  of the world's population this really is  now a global  emergency there's far more we can do we  send 600 pieces of surplus equipment  over  on sunday but we could do far more  particularly  the uk is a world leader in genome  sequencing and epidemiology  we can map and track variants of the  virus which will not only help  to get what is happening in india under  control but also help to make sure that  those variants don't spread to other  parts of the world  including the uk we need a proper plan  from the government about how they're  going to step up and tackle this global  emergency and that's what i'll be asking  dominic rob today i mean we have this  very strange situation where  we are providing them whether they're  buying the astrozenica vaccine but  effectively it cost it would seem and  yet  we seem to be buying five million doses  of vaccine  from india should we be doing that well  one of the questions i'll be asking  dominic rob today is do we have surplus  doses of the vaccine that we could use  to help  but the immediate problem in india is  they don't even have the staff  the medical staff to to administer the  vaccine they're desperately short of  oxygen of oxygen canisters of  ventilators  they've asked them us for that now do we  have surplus drugs that we could provide  because some of those drugs are going on  the black market for enormous  sums of money there is very very  immediate help that we could send we  need a global agreement on vaccine  surpluses  when we've got surplus doses and we will  have at some point  in this cycle we need to make sure that  we send them don't forget  a year ago we appealed for help to the  world because we were so woefully  unprepared  for this pandemic we had a prime  minister who'd just been released  from hospital himself suffering from  kovid and who stepped forward  it was the indian government who  released the export of  three million packets of paracetamol to  the uk because we'd run out  they stepped up for us in our hour of  need and we absolutely need to step up  for them i mean the transport secretary  telling us that  you know that we have sent over  equipment for for oxygen concentrators  you know we should pull oxygen supplies  out of the atmosphere  i mean that clearly is a step in the  right direction but you think do you  that there is actually a lot more we can  do  we sent 600 pieces of equipment it was  the right thing to do  but we haven't seen anywhere near the  peak of this crisis  in india experts predict that it's going  to be three weeks before we see  the peak of what's happening and we're  already seeing people turned away from  hospital  dying in hospital car parks crematoriums  overwhelmed people  burning bodies in their back gardens we  can't afford to step back now we should  be working with the united states who've  already  sent supplies over germany and other  countries in order to coordinate our  efforts and do  far far more in terms of that  coordination i mean  does the uk need to be seen as not just  a provider of aid but a  a broker of aid i think there's there  are diplomatic efforts that we could  deploy  to make sure that india is getting the  supplies that they need  particularly in the the region there's  more that we could do to prove  to ensure that supplies are sent very  very quickly  we also need to help india ramp up its  manufacturing capacity  it's been a huge debate about whether  more doses of the vaccine should be  donated  by wealthier countries who have lots of  those doses as you suggested  on order but the truth is if we end up  in a situation  where we're pitting india against  britain and countries against one  another  we're all going to lose to eradicate  this virus we need to eradicate it  everywhere and that means that we need  to ramp up our manufacturing  capability in countries like india and  the uk government could offer expertise  and help  to the indian government in order to  make sure that that happens lisa and  andy it's good to see you this morning  thank you

